Loser McSuck XI's parents obviously didn't like him very much.  Maybe
that's why they sent him into the Dungeons of Doom.  But he showed
them.  He showed them all.

Loser was lucky enough to find an altar on level 3, and established his
first stash there.  That room would serve as his base of operations for
a good part of his quest, and it got rather messy by the time all was
said and done.
He had the good fortune to find two blessed magic lamps in Minetown, as
well as a co-aligned altar, and soon had a blessed +2 gray dragon scale
mail and a blessed fireproof +2 pair of speed boots.  He also found a
+4 ring of protection and a cloak of displacement early, which aided
him greatly.
Loser's strange luck continued as he picked up a potion of speed off an
ogre's tattered corpse on the Home level and, seeing a crowd of ogres
coming, decided it couldn't hurt to take a sip.  Out came a genie, who
gave him a pair of blessed +2 rustproof gauntlets of power.  Thrones
granted him two genocides (mind flayers and rust monsters,) and a
throne wish got him a blessed scroll of genocide (L and ;.)  He would
later author two scrolls of genocides himself, which knocked
disenchanters and master mind flayers dead.
Unfortunately, not all went well for poor Loser.  While waiting in his
base for foolish enemies to come before the Cleaver and be served up to
Set, he was bitten by a werejackal and infected with lycanthropy.
Stuck in jackal form, with 1/4 HP remaining, he decided that being a
werejackal wasn't his thing, and offered up a prayer for help.

You feel much better.

Gee, thanks a lot Set.
Another prayer was apparently too soon, as Set was none too pleased.
Fortunately, Loser remembered his holy water stash and was able to
purge himself, but the problem remained of mollifying his god.  In his
desperation, he went to Minetown and had his pet troll kill the one
remaining shopkeeper and the priest; neither left a corpse.  Set would
not be appeased until a dragon happened to fall across the altar at the
bottom of the Quest.
Loser was now almost ready to storm the castle, but one problem
remained--he had piles upon piles of scrolls and potions in his stash,
but no identify identified.  Taking a leap of faith, he took the most
common scroll in the stash (besides teleportation, which he had
identified,) doffed his armor, put any flammables away from him and
read the scroll.  To his delight, it was identify, and between the
eight scrolls he soon had most of his stash identified--including
enchant weapon/armor.  This was where his second big blunder
occurred--he read a blessed scroll of enchant armor and his already +4
gray dragon scale mail evaporated.  After a storm of cursing, he
remembered the gray dragon he had slain a few levels down, and picked
up its scales, but, alas, without any enchant armor remaining, or even
a magic marker, he would have to assault the castle with a simple set
of gray dragon scales as armor.
Interestingly enough, while sacrificing at an altar somewhere in the
tens, he accidentally served up one of his orcish brethren to Set, and
wouldn't you know it, Juiblex appeared.  Of course, Dragort the yellow
dragon made short work of him, but was later lost to a master mind
flayer.  In his memory, Loser brought Dragort II the red dragon to the
castle.  Dragort II fought valiantly, but ultimately fell before tooth,
claw and blade.  Yet his sacrifice was not in vain, and Loser claimed
the castle--and its wand of wishing--for his own.  He soon had his
Frost Brand up to +6 and the silver saber, which he affectionately
named Pimpcane, up to +7, and all his armor erodeproofed.
Before taking the plunge into Gehennom, Loser decided to move his stash
to the coaligned church on level 19 (where he would later max his
protection with the spoils of Gehennom.)  Bringing his chest of stuff
down 16 levels was an arduous task that almost ended in disaster when
he took the wrong staircase and wound up in the Mines without any way
to carry the chest back up, but fortunately, his stash contained one
cursed potion of gain level.
Loser struck Gehennom hard and fast, and no one could stop him as he
hacked his way to the Amulet.  Well, except those accursed gremlins.
Though they were peaceful, they multiplied like proverbial rabbits and
soon filled the Juiblex level (well, that and the castle moat, but
Dragort II performed excellent crowd control) with green g's from
corner to corner of the screen.  Loser was eventually forced to
blessed-genocide g.  Anyway, the trip back up was relatively speedy,
with only a few visits from the Wizard.  Loser hurried through the
Elemental Planes, only occasionally stopping to say hi to the
indigenous wildlife, and soon found himself on the Astral Plane.
Although Death shaved off 30 of his maximum hitpoints, Loser persevered
through the trek to the Lawful altar... then the Neutral altar... and
finally the Chaotic altar.  Ants hanging off him and dragons and
priests chasing him through the door, Loser threw himself upon the
altar to Set and offered up the Amulet...

